  "explanation": {
    "id": "fc983343-5f9c-44bb-a8e1-9436e0f2f4fd",
    "body": "The video has an error despite obtaining the same answer.\n\nThe row reduction should be R3 = R3 - 3*R1, not R3 = R3 - 2*R1. This means that the resulting matrix after the two row reduction steps (second line) should be:\n\n$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc}1 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ c-2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & c-6 & 0 & 0 \\end{array}\\right]\n$\n\nHere, it is then clear that for $c=2$ , this matrix reduces to\n\n$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc}1 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 0 \\end{array}\\right] -> \\left[\\begin{array}{cccc}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{array}\\right]\n$\n\nwhich has one free variable (the third column). This corresponds to a nullity of 1. The same goes for  $c=6$ . The matrix reduces to:\n\n$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{array}\\right] ->\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc}0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{array}\\right] \n$\n\nwith row reduction. Again, this system has one free variable (the third column), corresponding to a nullity of 1. \n\nThe answer is E.\n",
    "questionId": "f3899e34-655d-4fcb-b453-a73cdca833c0",
